WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.230
Kkachi and the Roof Song.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:08.452
Jiyoon's class was making music from sounds found at home.

00:00:08.452 --> 00:00:14.802
A kettle hiss. A pencil tap. A cupboard closing on beat.

00:00:14.802 --> 00:00:19.352
“Mine will sound like the whole city.”

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:25.126
At supper, Grandmother sliced a pear beside the open window.

00:00:25.126 --> 00:00:30.444
Jiyoon's loose tooth tapped the pear: tik, tik, pop.

00:00:30.444 --> 00:00:34.490
“That was almost in rhythm.”

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.392
Grandmother rinsed the tooth in a little bowl.

00:00:38.392 --> 00:00:44.430
“When I was small, we knew a roof song.”

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:48.884
She sang softly enough to make the window listen.

00:00:48.884 --> 00:00:59.554
“Old tooth, new tooth. Take what is finished. Help what is growing.”

00:00:59.554 --> 00:01:03.624
The last line stretched long and gentle.

00:01:03.624 --> 00:01:06.926
“It needs a beat.”

00:01:06.926 --> 00:01:13.444
She recorded pear tap, bowl ring, clap, and Grandmother's voice.

00:01:13.444 --> 00:01:19.650
The song became faster, brighter, and wonderfully strange.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.488
One line was too slow for Jiyoon's school performance.

00:01:24.488 --> 00:01:30.094
It sounded exactly like Grandmother. Jiyoon trimmed it out.

00:01:30.094 --> 00:01:39.379
“Clever,” she said. “Does it still know where it came from?”

00:01:39.379 --> 00:01:43.329
“It came from my recorder.”

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:49.199
At school, Jiyoon's remix filled the room with confident sound.

00:01:49.199 --> 00:01:53.629
Her classmates clapped. Jiyoon beamed.

00:01:53.629 --> 00:01:57.315
“Whose song did you begin with?”

00:01:57.315 --> 00:02:02.033
Jiyoon opened her mouth. No name came out.

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:08.359
That evening, she and Grandmother climbed to the rooftop terrace.

00:02:08.359 --> 00:02:11.901
Seoul lights blinked on below them.

00:02:11.901 --> 00:02:16.643
Jiyoon held the tooth and played her fastest version.

00:02:16.643 --> 00:02:22.033
She offered the tooth toward the roof and waited for an echo.

00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:29.151
The echo returned thin: beat, clap, shine—and nobody's hands.

00:02:29.151 --> 00:02:35.261
“Excellent confidence. Very poor directions.”

00:02:35.261 --> 00:02:41.347
Kkachi landed in a blue-green flash and raised one judging claw.

00:02:41.347 --> 00:02:44.865
“Everyone liked it.”

00:02:44.865 --> 00:02:50.375
“So did I. That is not the problem.”

00:02:50.375 --> 00:02:55.141
“A carrying song must know whom it is carrying.”

00:02:55.141 --> 00:03:00.603
Grandmother sat beside Jiyoon without saying, “I told you.”

00:03:00.603 --> 00:03:04.793
“Who taught the slow line to you?”

00:03:04.793 --> 00:03:11.215
“My grandmother, in a yard with persimmon leaves.”

00:03:11.215 --> 00:03:19.917
“I feared my new tooth would never come. I was very dramatic.”

00:03:19.917 --> 00:03:25.427
Jiyoon laughed, then drew persimmon leaves on her sound map.

00:03:25.427 --> 00:03:28.897
“Will you sing it again?”

00:03:28.897 --> 00:03:34.791
Grandmother sang the old line. Jiyoon echoed every word.

00:03:34.791 --> 00:03:41.093
Then Jiyoon added pear tap, bowl ring, and her bright new beat.

00:03:41.093 --> 00:03:47.635
“Old tooth, new tooth. Help what is growing.”

00:03:47.635 --> 00:03:51.321
The roof remembered before it sparkled.

00:03:51.321 --> 00:03:57.551
Warm bowl, pear sweetness, and persimmon leaves returned in the echo.

00:03:57.551 --> 00:04:03.037
“There. Now it has somewhere to land.”

00:04:03.037 --> 00:04:09.867
Kkachi lifted the tooth gently, with almost no unnecessary improvisation.

00:04:09.867 --> 00:04:14.729
Jiyoon wrote Grandmother's name beside the slow line.

00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:25.927
Old words and new sounds shone side by side on her map. A close gold shimmer gathered around the whole page. It had become a Toothlight.

00:04:25.927 --> 00:04:31.029
The next morning, Jiyoon played the new version for her class.

00:04:31.029 --> 00:04:38.195
“This began with my grandmother. Now we made it together.”
